-- title:
--   The rank certificate holds for every eight-bit mask
-- statement:
--   Write $C=\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/4\mathbb Z$, with residue map $\rho$ taking $(0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(0,3),(1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)$ to $1,7,19,13,11,17,29,23$, respectively. For every natural mask $0\le m<256$, its specified rank $r(m)$ is at most five, equals five exactly when residue one is present, and for every $g\in C$ its specified subproduct update $s_g$ satisfies
--
--   $$\min(r(m)+1,5)\le r(s_g(m)).$$
--
--   These are the complete finite rank-certificate properties on all eight-bit masks.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, Certified minimal memory for a divisor-residue presence observable modulo 30, https://zenodo.org/records/23989640; archive Couret_BC_MOD30_Companion_20261105.zip, SHA-256 9bb2678164c63a9e47c192093f5ce9deb1b7b74a1f1c8fecc77f305a408266a2; file source/S5/RankChecks.lean, lines 22–23; source file SHA-256 b8d60d7ded486a808030beca51d8511969230b1e678abc7a67c0f65b3a068232; theorem Couret.S5.rank_facts

